Here are a few ideas of on what you might comment on:
How would you rate the quality of the output and why?
What SystemVerilog constructs/syntax did the LLM generated that were new to you?
Did the LLM-generated code synthesize the first time around? If not, what were the issues?
What error or warning messages did the synthesizer/simulator produce?
What would you do differently the next time you use an LLM in your workflow?
I think the quality of the output from the LLM was alright. One interesting thing is that the AI output is similar for both models without having the context of AES. It might be because I am using the fast version and it auto-generates a response and might not read the PDF. This could be because I was using the faster Flash model, which might have relied more on recognizing common coding patterns rather than carefully interpreting the PDF and pseudocode I provided. However, both responses seemed to understand the general structure of the algorithm and translated it into reasonable SystemVerilog.
In the future, I would probably use an LLM more as a starting point rather than expecting it to generate a complete solution. I would also provide more specific instructions about the module interfaces, hardware architecture, and SystemVerilog constructs I wanted it to use. Instead of asking it to generate the entire algorithm at once, I think it is more useful to break the problem into smaller components and verify each one independently. Overall, the LLM was helpful for understanding how to translate an algorithm into hardware, but I would not trust its output.

